        "text": "The idea that microbes manipulate what you want to eat comes from [a 2014 paper in BioEssays by Alcock and colleagues](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4270213/). It is a good paper and it is worth reading. But it is a hypothesis paper — the authors set out the evolutionary logic and the mechanisms that could plausibly do it, including microbes acting on reward and satiety pathways, changing taste receptors, and signalling up the vagus nerve. What it is not is a demonstration that this happens in people. It argues that it could. Twelve years on, that is still roughly where things stand."
