        "text": "The most concrete version of the idea involves a bacterial protein called ClpB, which happens to look like a fragment of α-MSH — one of your own appetite-suppressing signals. [Work published in the International Journal of Obesity in 2020](https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7188665/) showed that a ClpB-producing gut bacterium, Hafnia alvei, reduced food intake, weight gain and fat mass in obese mice, and that a ClpB-deficient E. coli strain did not have the effect. That is a clean result — in mice. The human half of that paper was a computer analysis of stool samples from 569 people, not a trial."
