        "text": "The first is energy harvest — whether your bacteria extract more calories from the same plate. This comes from [Turnbaugh and colleagues in Nature in 2006](https://www.nature.com/articles/nature05414), the study that launched the whole field. They showed that the microbiota of obese mice had a greater capacity to release energy from food, and — the part that made it famous — that transplanting that microbiota into germ-free mice caused significantly more body fat gain than transplanting a lean one. It is a genuinely important result. It is also mice, and the size of the effect in free-living humans eating varied diets has never been pinned down the way the headlines imply."
