        "text": "The second is transit time — how fast things physically move through you — and here the evidence points somewhere most articles do not expect. [A 2016 study in Nature Microbiology](https://www.nature.com/articles/nmicrobiol201693) measured colonic transit time directly, with markers you can see on an X-ray, and found that a longer transit time went with a richer, more diverse microbiome. Slower, not faster. But the same study found that with a long transit time the colon shifts from fermenting carbohydrate to breaking down protein, producing metabolites that are not obviously good for you. The authors' own conclusion is the useful bit: a high microbial richness does not automatically mean a healthy gut."
