    "faq": [
      {
        "q": "Can gut bacteria really make me crave sugar?",
        "a": "Not in the way the phrase suggests. The idea comes from a 2014 hypothesis paper that set out how microbes could influence appetite — through reward and satiety pathways, taste receptors and the vagus nerve — and it remains a hypothesis in humans. The most concrete evidence is a bacterial protein, ClpB, that resembles one of your own appetite-suppressing hormones, and the work showing it reduces food intake was done in mice. A human trial of a ClpB-producing probiotic did help 236 dieting adults lose slightly more weight than placebo, but everybody in it was also on a calorie deficit. Your bacteria are one input among many, not the driver."
      },
      {
        "q": "Is there a test that tells me whether my gut bacteria are good or bad?",
        "a": "Not a useful one. The most-quoted measure, the Firmicutes-to-Bacteroidetes ratio, was reviewed in 2020 and found so inconsistent across studies that it cannot be treated as a marker of anything. Even microbial diversity, which sounds like it must be good, is not straightforwardly so — the study that measured gut transit time directly found high diversity alongside a less favourable pattern of gut metabolism. If you are managing blood sugar, your own meter or sensor tells you far more that is actionable than any commercial stool test currently does."
      },
      {
        "q": "Should I take a probiotic for my blood sugar?",
        "a": "Only with your diabetes team, and with realistic expectations. Two specific products have trials: pasteurised Akkermansia muciniphila improved insulin sensitivity in a pilot of 32 people, and a five-strain formulation modestly lowered post-meal glucose in 76 people with type 2 diabetes at a p-value right on the line, with no change in inflammation. Nothing here is a substitute for medication, and if you take insulin or a sulfonylurea, anything that lowers your glucose is a dose conversation before it is a supplement decision. Food — plant variety and fermented foods — has better evidence than capsules do."
      },
      {
        "q": "How quickly does the gut microbiome change, and does it last?",
        "a": "Fast, and no. Dietary changes shift the composition of gut bacteria within days, and it drifts back towards where it was when you stop. In the seventeen-week fermented-foods trial, diversity climbed steadily while people were eating that way. That is the honest framing: this is a habit rather than a course of treatment, which also means a difficult fortnight does no lasting harm."
      }
    ]
